 are drawn **side by side** wherever they share a road, metro-map style, each in its                                                                 [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 232 +[38;2;248;248;242mown colour; a stretch used by one line is a single line centred on the road. The geometry is the                                                                  [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 233 +[38;2;248;248;242mpure function `bundleRoutes(shapes)` in `src/domain/routeBundles.ts`; `MapView` only renders it.                                                                  [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 234 +                                                                                                                                                                  [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 235 +[38;2;248;248;242m- **Overlap means identical vertices.** The source's shapes share exactly identical vertices                                                                      [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 236 +  [38;2;248;248;242mwhere lines overlap, so overlap is found by matching edges (pairs of consecutive vertices,                                                                      [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 237 +  [38;2;248;248;242mkeyed to 6 decimal places, direction ignored), with no geometric tolerance. Stretches whose                                                                     [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 238 +  [38;2;248;248;242mvertices don't match exactly are simply drawn un-offset, overlapping as they would without                                                                      [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 239 +  [38;2;248;248;242mbundling. If a future source digitises shared roads differently, this is where it degrades.                                                                     [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 240 +[38;2;248;248;242m- **Per line, not per shape.** A line's outbound and inbound shapes form one strand: each edge of                                                                 [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 241 +  [38;2;248;248;242ma line is drawn exactly once, whichever direction it comes from. Where a direction diverges                                                                     [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 242 +  [38;2;248;248;242m(U3), its own edges are drawn too.                                                                                                                              [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 243 +[38;2;248;248;242m- **Slots.** On an edge carrying *n* lines, lines are ordered by id with plain string comparison                                                                  [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 244 +  [38;2;248;248;242m(`S1, S2, U1, U2, U3`) and get slots `i − (n−1)/2` (−0.5/+0.5 for two, −1/0/+1 for three…),                                                                     [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 245 +  [38;2;248;248;242mleft to right relative to the edge's orientation.                                                                                                               [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 246 +[38;2;248;248;242m- **Orientation.** MapLibre's `line-offset` is relative to each line's drawing direction, and the                                                                 [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 247 +  [38;2;248;248;242mshapes are digitised in either direction. So every edge gets one consistent orientation,                                                                        [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 248 +  [38;2;248;248;242mgrown greedily from the busiest edge, always continuing into the neighbour that shares the                                                                      [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 249 +  [38;2;248;248;242mmost lines; this keeps each line on the same side along a corridor and through junctions where                                                                  [K[m            [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 250 +  [38;2;248;248;242mthe bundle continues. Each run's offset is then signed against its own travel direction.                                                                        [K[m       [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m[23;6H 251 +[38;2;248;248;242m- **Runs.** The output is a list of `LineRun`s (`line`, `color`, `offset` in line widths,[73X[m[24;3H   [38;2;80;200;80m[48;2;2;40;0m 252 +  [38;2;248;248;242m`points`): consecutive edges of one line with the same offset. Where a line joins or leaves a[67X[38;2;80;200;80m[25;6H 253 +  [38;2;248;248;242mbundle its offset changes and a new run starts at the shared vertex; the small sideways jog[69X[m
